From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 07:16:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08875/11362] exp6255: the win exemplar makes dynamics
 induction WORSE (1 improved, 3 worse)

REQ-ARC-WMTE-6255. Pooled delta -0.2476. Not inert -- actively harmful. ls20 is
the clearest case: a near-perfect engine (0.9038 held-out) collapsed to 0.0678
when the exemplar was added. dc22 -0.4285, cn04 -0.1095, s5i5 +0.3834.

This one is well-controlled, unlike two earlier runs in this batch. Four of four
games comparable with no skips, all eight induce calls returned ok, and no game
sits at the 0.0 floor or the 1.0 ceiling -- controls span 0.147 to 0.904 and
treatments 0.037 to 0.892, so every game had room to move in both directions.
Paired on an identical split and budget; the only variable is whether
win_transition and previous_level_complete_grid were passed.

THE LIMITATION THAT MAY INVERT THIS READING. The metric scores the ENGINE, i.e.
dynamics fidelity. The win exemplar's purpose is arguably the GOAL predicate,
which this did not score at all. So the honest claim is: the exemplar hurts
dynamics induction, and its effect on goal induction is UNMEASURED. It may well
help the thing it was designed for while degrading the thing measured here. That
follow-up is cheap and is the obvious next step on this axis.

Scope limit unchanged: the exemplar is replayed from a banked solve through a
GameAdapter, a development proxy. A hidden game has neither, so this says nothing
about the live agent obtaining an exemplar on a hidden level 1. n=4, far below the
n>=30 bar.

Consequence for the standing diagnosis: the empty win-exemplar slot was the best
remaining explanation for offline induction sitting near 0.38-0.5. Filling it
lowers those numbers rather than raising them, so that hypothesis is dead and the
induction-quality wall stands unexplained. Session tally on induction-adjacent
levers: search structure null, sampling breadth null, goal gradient null, model
architecture null, win exemplar negative.

